---------------------------------------- Lazy loading ----------------------------------------------

- LAZY-LOAD RACE AND USER ROUTES, INSPECT THE GENERATED BUNDLES, AND ENABLE PRELOADING.

- Instruction: Angular Prerequisite "Router" chapter (Advanced routing section)

1️⃣ Our application has grown in size quite a bit since the beginning. You can have an idea of the
    size of the app by running:

        ng build --named-chunks
    
    👉 ng build builds the app with the production configuration, pre-compiles the templates and 
    then minifies the result. It also adds a unique SHA1 to the files: it’s a feature known as
    "cache-busting", as it guarantees that your users will never have a stale resource when you
    redeploy your application. It also enables special optimizations to have a better tree-shaking,
    resulting in smaller files. Tree-shaking and dead-code elimination are the features that remove
    the unneeded portions of the code after a static analysis.

    👉 --named-chunks will give nice names to the files when you have lazy-loaded routes
    (races.routes-xxx.js instead of chunk-xxx.js).

    You can explore the dist/ponyracer/browser directory. It will contain all the necessary assets
    for the application. The ones that interest us the most are the main JS files, that the
    ng build command lists:

        Initial chunk files | Names         |  Raw size | Estimated transfer size
        main-PCLFI7CR.js    | main          | 384.31 kB |               102.76 kB
        styles-5G46XTEI.css | styles        | 310.29 kB |                39.56 kB

                            | Initial total | 694.60 kB |               142.32 kB
    
    main-xxxx.js contains the app logic and the 3rd party libraries (like Angular, RxJS, date-fns…​).
    The styles file contains the CSS needed by the application.

    You may not have exactly the same results, but that should be in this ball-park. It means that
    when users load our application for the first time, they get 694.60 kB of JavaScript, which is
    quite a bit to download on a poor mobile connection.

    To be more accurate, we should measure the file sizes when they are gzipped. Browsers won’t
    mind if they receive a gzipped version of the file: they are able to unzip it and go on. And
    it saves us a lot of bandwidth (and loading time), as you can see in the right column of the
    build result (Estimated Transfer Size). 🔥With compression enabled, we go from 694.60 kB to
    142.32 kB! That’s good, but can we do even better 🤔?

    ✅🔥Yes we can. Because right now, these files contain the whole application, whereas a user
    probably doesn’t need every single component, pipe, service and 3rd party library for the first
    page!

    🔥🔥We can split the application in chunks, and lazy-load those chunks when they’re really
    needed.



2️⃣ OK let’s go! The biggest win is probably to use lazy-loading for all the race related business,
    and only load them when the user navigates to a URL starting with /races.

    Now we need to make these routes a part of our application. To do so, we need to:
    👉 create a file named races.routes.ts in the races directory
    👉 extract all the children routes of /races in app.routes.ts, and add them in races.routes.ts,
    export them as racesRoutes
    👉 in app.routes.ts, replace the children key with a loadChildren key The value of loadChildren
    should be the function () => import('./races/races.routes').then(m => m.racesRoutes).

    races.routes.ts:

     👉 export const racesRoutes: Routes = [
            { path: '', component: Races,
                children: [
                { path: '', pathMatch: 'full', redirectTo: 'pending' },
                { path: 'pending', component: PendingRaces },
                { path: 'finished', component: FinishedRaces }
                ]
            },
            { path: ':raceId', component: Bet },
            { path: ':raceId/live', component: Live }
        ];
    

    app.routes.ts:

        export const routes: Routes = [
            { path: '', component: Home },
            {
                path: 'races',
                canActivate: [loggedInGuard],
             👉 loadChildren: () => import('./races/races.routes').then(m => m.racesRoutes)
            },
            { path: 'login', component: Login },
            { path: 'register', component: Register }
        ];


    🔥Now, whenever a user will click on a link starting with /races, a JavaScript file containing all
    the races related components/pipes/services will be fetched.

    If you rebuild the application (ng build --named-chunks), you should now have a new file named
    like races.routes-xxx.js: this is the chunk extracted by the CLI. Let’s have a look at the sizes:

        Initial chunk files      | Names         |  Raw size | Estimated transfer size
        styles-5G46XTEI.css      | styles        | 310.29 kB |                39.56 kB
        chunk-NLFYUZV3.js        | -             | 290.44 kB |                80.59 kB
        main-IKLAGFUW.js         | main          |  72.64 kB |                18.13 kB

                                 | Initial total | 673.37 kB |               138.28 kB 👈

        Lazy chunk files         | Names         |  Raw size | Estimated transfer size
        races.routes-YMHFYYGL.js | races-routes  |  22.99 kB |                 7.01 kB
    
    ✅The total size is a bit more, but now the first page will only load 138.28 kB worth of JavaScript,
    which is quite a nice gain!



3️⃣ Let’s explore the bundles generated. 🔥A nice tool called source-map-explorer allows to generate a
    diagram of what is inside the bundle.

    Rebuild the application with the sourcemaps:

        ng build --named-chunks --source-map
    
    And explore the bundles:

        npx source-map-explorer dist/ponyracer/browser/races.routes-xxx.js --no-border-checks
    
    ✅This should open your browser and display the content of the bundle. 😶‍🌫️As you will spot, part of
    the bundle is date-fns. 🧠🧠Indeed, as we moved the component that uses FromNowPipe into the
    lazy-loaded route, the CLI has now packaged date-fns into the chunk instead of into the main
    bundle. (see the image racesRoutesMap.png)

    ⚠️The main chunk still contain useless stuff for the first page, like all the "users" related
    components: Register and Login. (see the image mainMap.png)

        npx source-map-explorer dist/ponyracer/browser/main-xxx.js --no-border-checks
    
    ⚠️More importantly, these components are responsible for the inclusion of the forms APIs from
    @angular/forms/signals. And you can see that these forms-related dependencies are quite a big
    part of the main bundle.

    🔥🔥We can check manually which components use the package @angular/forms/signals, or, even
    better, use a tool to help us! Run the following command:

        npx @rx-angular/import-graph-visualizer --entry-points ./src/main.ts
    
    ✅This should open your browser with a nice interface that allows you to explore the import graph
    of your application. Enter @angular/forms/signals in the Import target field, and you will see
    that BirthYearInput, Register and Login are the only components that use it.
    (see importGraphVisualizer.png)



